.SH "Unit Tests and Functional Tests"
.PP
.SS "In general XP"
A Unit \fBTest\fP is designed to test the interface of a single class through proper pass/fail criteria\&. A Functional \fBTest\fP tests the result of class interactions within a bigger context\&. Hence, pass/fail criteria are more complex\&. This also includes performance test and the validation of applications in their whole\&.
.SS "Interpreted for CSMP"
To test classes that require a model to act on, multiple Unit Tests may be run within one test function and work with the same model\&. Classes are tested indepently from another, and the \fBTestSuite\fP will spot failures individually\&. To sum it up, we put multiple tests inside a single test function that establishes a model for all of them\&. This is done to reduce the effort that goes into creating a test\&. Functional Tests, or \fBTest\fP Cases, measure performance and compare results of complex applications\&.
.SH "Introduction to TDD"
.PP
Write your Unit \fBTest\fP first, let it fail(because there is no class yet, afterall) then implement your class piecewise to make the tests pass\&. Once the tests pass, improve your code\&. Benefits:
.IP "\(bu" 2
improves design
.IP "  \(bu" 4
think it through
.IP "  \(bu" 4
then implement
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
reduces amount of code
.IP "  \(bu" 4
by sticking to what you really planned to do
.IP "  \(bu" 4
by having code that does the job quickly
.IP "    \(bu" 6
and improve it when there's time
.PP

.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
used together with SVN, often replaces debugger
.IP "  \(bu" 4
